{:edb/db-before-t 192 :edb/db-after-t 193 :edb/tx-data [[13194139533505 50 #inst "2026-10-11T03:52:49.151Z" 13194139533505 17592186406335 true] [17592186405040 1004 #edb/ref 17592186406336 13194139533505 17592186406335 true] [17592186405040 1004 #edb/ref 17592186406337 13194139533505 17592186406335 true] [17592186405040 1004 #edb/ref 17592186406338 13194139533505 17592186406335 true] [17592186405040 1004 #edb/ref 17592186406339 13194139533505 17592186406335 true] [17592186405040 1004 #edb/ref 17592186406340 13194139533505 17592186406335 true] [17592186405040 1004 #edb/ref 17592186406341 13194139533505 17592186406335 true] [17592186405040 1004 #edb/ref 17592186406342 13194139533505 17592186406335 true] [17592186405040 1004 #edb/ref 17592186406343 13194139533505 17592186406335 true] [17592186405040 1004 #edb/ref 17592186406344 13194139533505 17592186406335 true] [17592186405040 1007 "Differential Calculus introduces the mathematics of change for real-valued functions of one variable. Building on algebra and trigonometry, the course develops the concepts and methods needed to interpret derivatives, analyze functions, and solve problems involving changing quantities. Graphical, numerical, and symbolic representations connect computational techniques with mathematical reasoning and applications." 13194139533505 17592186406335 true] [17592186405040 1008 "Limits and continuity provide the conceptual framework for studying derivatives. Students connect average rates of change with instantaneous rates, then use limit laws and differentiation rules—including the chain rule—to calculate and interpret derivatives. The emphasis is on understanding what these calculations represent, rather than treating differentiation solely as algebraic manipulation.\n\nApplications connect derivatives to motion, the shape of a graph, and maximum and minimum problems. Students use calculus to analyze velocity, speed, and acceleration; investigate how functions change; and solve optimization problems. Mathematical modeling includes identifying relevant variables, making appropriate assumptions, and evaluating whether a method and its conclusions make sense in context." 13194139533505 17592186406335 true] [17592186405041 1004 #edb/ref 17592186406389 13194139533505 17592186406335 true] [17592186405041 1004 #edb/ref 17592186406390 13194139533505 17592186406335 true] [17592186405041 1004 #edb/ref 17592186406391 13194139533505 17592186406335 true] [17592186405041 1004 #edb/ref 17592186406392 13194139533505 17592186406335 true] [17592186405041 1004 #edb/ref 17592186406393 13194139533505 17592186406335 true] [17592186405041 1004 #edb/ref 17592186406394 13194139533505 17592186406335 true] [17592186405041 1004 #edb/ref 17592186406395 13194139533505 17592186406335 true] [17592186405041 1004 #edb/ref 17592186406396 13194139533505 17592186406335 true] [17592186405041 1004 #edb/ref 17592186406397 13194139533505 17592186406335 true] [17592186405041 1007 "Integral Calculus studies accumulation and integration for functions of one variable. Building on differential calculus, the course develops definite and indefinite integrals and their applications. Graphical, numerical, and symbolic approaches support the interpretation of integrals and their use in mathematical modeling and problem solving." 13194139533505 17592186406335 true] [17592186405041 1008 "The course connects Riemann sums, definite integrals, and antiderivatives through the Fundamental Theorem of Calculus. Students develop integration techniques and use numerical approximation alongside analytical methods. These tools support applications to areas, volumes, curve lengths, motion, force, and work. Logarithmic and exponential functions, polar coordinates, improper integrals, and elementary growth and decay models extend the range of problems addressed." 13194139533505 17592186406335 true] [17592186405042 1004 #edb/ref 17592186406355 13194139533505 17592186406335 true] [17592186405042 1004 #edb/ref 17592186406356 13194139533505 17592186406335 true] [17592186405042 1004 #edb/ref 17592186406357 13194139533505 17592186406335 true] [17592186405042 1004 #edb/ref 17592186406358 13194139533505 17592186406335 true] [17592186405042 1004 #edb/ref 17592186406359 13194139533505 17592186406335 true] [17592186405042 1004 #edb/ref 17592186406360 13194139533505 17592186406335 true] [17592186405042 1004 #edb/ref 17592186406361 13194139533505 17592186406335 true] [17592186405042 1004 #edb/ref 17592186406362 13194139533505 17592186406335 true] [17592186405042 1004 #edb/ref 17592186406363 13194139533505 17592186406335 true] [17592186405042 1004 #edb/ref 17592186406364 13194139533505 17592186406335 true] [17592186405042 1004 #edb/ref 17592186406365 13194139533505 17592186406335 true] [17592186405042 1004 #edb/ref 17592186406366 13194139533505 17592186406335 true] [17592186405042 1004 #edb/ref 17592186406367 13194139533505 17592186406335 true] [17592186405042 1007 "Vector Calculus I extends differentiation and integration to vector-valued functions and functions of several variables. Building on single-variable calculus, the course develops mathematical tools for describing curves, surfaces, motion, and quantities distributed across regions in two or three dimensions. Geometric interpretation and physical applications connect vector operations, multivariable derivatives, and multiple integrals." 13194139533505 17592186406335 true] [17592186405042 1008 "Vectors and coordinate systems provide a language for describing geometry in the plane and in space. Vector-valued functions represent curves and motion, while derivatives and integrals of these functions describe velocity, acceleration, arc length, and curvature. Equations of surfaces and their level sets extend graphical reasoning to functions of several variables.\n\nMultivariable differentiation includes limits, continuity, partial derivatives, the chain rule, directional derivatives, and gradients. These concepts support tangent-plane approximations and optimization, including constrained problems using Lagrange multipliers. Multiple integration addresses accumulated quantities over planar and spatial regions. Rectangular, polar, cylindrical, and spherical coordinates—and more general changes of variables—allow students to match an integral’s representation to the geometry of its region." 13194139533505 17592186406335 true] [17592186405043 1004 #edb/ref 17592186406345 13194139533505 17592186406335 true] [17592186405043 1004 #edb/ref 17592186406346 13194139533505 17592186406335 true] [17592186405043 1004 #edb/ref 17592186406347 13194139533505 17592186406335 true] [17592186405043 1004 #edb/ref 17592186406348 13194139533505 17592186406335 true] [17592186405043 1004 #edb/ref 17592186406349 13194139533505 17592186406335 true] [17592186405043 1004 #edb/ref 17592186406350 13194139533505 17592186406335 true] [17592186405043 1004 #edb/ref 17592186406351 13194139533505 17592186406335 true] [17592186405043 1004 #edb/ref 17592186406352 13194139533505 17592186406335 true] [17592186405043 1004 #edb/ref 17592186406353 13194139533505 17592186406335 true] [17592186405043 1004 #edb/ref 17592186406354 13194139533505 17592186406335 true] [17592186405043 1007 "Vector Calculus II develops the calculus of vector fields and integration along curves and over surfaces. Building on Vector Calculus I, the course connects local descriptions of a field with accumulated quantities such as work, circulation, and flux. Its central tools include conservative fields, divergence, curl, and the integral theorems of Gauss and Stokes, with applications involving force, work, mass, and charge." 13194139533505 17592186406335 true] [17592186405043 1008 "The course revisits vector functions, space curves, gradients, and directional derivatives before extending integration to curves and surfaces. Line integrals connect vector fields with work and circulation. Conservative fields, potential functions, and path independence explain when an integral can be determined from endpoint information rather than the details of a path.\n\nSurface geometry and oriented surface elements provide the foundation for surface integrals and flux. Divergence and curl describe local field behavior, while the Divergence Theorem and Stokes’ Theorem connect these local quantities to integrals over regions and their boundaries. The archived course materials emphasize geometric understanding and the use of vector differentials to connect the various integration methods." 13194139533505 17592186406335 true] [17592186405044 1004 #edb/ref 17592186406368 13194139533505 17592186406335 true] [17592186405044 1004 #edb/ref 17592186406369 13194139533505 17592186406335 true] [17592186405044 1004 #edb/ref 17592186406370 13194139533505 17592186406335 true] [17592186405044 1004 #edb/ref 17592186406371 13194139533505 17592186406335 true] [17592186405044 1004 #edb/ref 17592186406372 13194139533505 17592186406335 true] [17592186405044 1004 #edb/ref 17592186406373 13194139533505 17592186406335 true] [17592186405044 1004 #edb/ref 17592186406374 13194139533505 17592186406335 true] [17592186405044 1004 #edb/ref 17592186406375 13194139533505 17592186406335 true] [17592186405044 1004 #edb/ref 17592186406376 13194139533505 17592186406335 true] [17592186405044 1007 "Applied Differential Equations introduces methods for describing and solving problems in which an unknown function is related to its derivatives. The course focuses on ordinary differential equations and applications appropriate to science and engineering, particularly electrical circuits and mechanical oscillators. Coverage includes first-order linear and selected nonlinear equations, second-order linear equations, and introductory work with higher-order equations and Laplace transforms." 13194139533505 17592186406335 true] [17592186405044 1008 "The archived syllabus begins with mathematical modeling and the classification of differential equations, then develops methods for first-order and second-order equations. This organization connects the structure of an equation with an appropriate solution approach. Applications give meaning to the unknown functions and their derivatives rather than treating equations as purely formal expressions.\n\nLaplace transforms provide an additional approach to solving differential equations, while introductory higher-order work extends the range of equations considered. Electrical circuits and mechanical oscillators serve as central application settings. The published description also identifies familiarity with complex numbers and Euler’s identities as relevant background." 13194139533505 17592186406335 true] [17592186405045 1004 #edb/ref 17592186406377 13194139533505 17592186406335 true] [17592186405045 1004 #edb/ref 17592186406378 13194139533505 17592186406335 true] [17592186405045 1004 #edb/ref 17592186406379 13194139533505 17592186406335 true] [17592186405045 1004 #edb/ref 17592186406380 13194139533505 17592186406335 true] [17592186405045 1004 #edb/ref 17592186406381 13194139533505 17592186406335 true] [17592186405045 1004 #edb/ref 17592186406382 13194139533505 17592186406335 true] [17592186405045 1004 #edb/ref 17592186406383 13194139533505 17592186406335 true] [17592186405045 1004 #edb/ref 17592186406384 13194139533505 17592186406335 true] [17592186405045 1004 #edb/ref 17592186406385 13194139533505 17592186406335 true] [17592186405045 1004 #edb/ref 17592186406386 13194139533505 17592186406335 true] [17592186405045 1004 #edb/ref 17592186406387 13194139533505 17592186406335 true] [17592186405045 1004 #edb/ref 17592186406388 13194139533505 17592186406335 true] [17592186405045 1007 "Linear Algebra I introduces the computational and conceptual study of linear systems, matrices, and subspaces. The course connects methods for solving equations with the structure of linear transformations and vector spaces. Determinants, linear independence, bases, dimension, and introductory eigenvalue analysis provide complementary ways to understand how a linear system or transformation behaves." 13194139533505 17592186406335 true] [17592186405045 1008 "Work with linear systems and Gaussian elimination establishes a foundation for matrix operations, invertibility, and determinants. The course then connects these calculations with linear independence, spanning sets, subspaces, bases, and dimension. Row, column, and null spaces describe important features of a matrix, while rank–nullity relates those spaces to the structure of a system’s solutions.\n\nMatrix representations make linear transformations concrete through examples such as rotation, reflection, projection, and scaling. Eigenvalues and eigenvectors introduce directions preserved by a transformation, including examples with complex eigenvalues. The archived section used here also introduces diagonalization and illustrates eigenvalue methods through simple systems of differential equations." 13194139533505 17592186406335 true] [17592186406336 1013 7 13194139533505 17592186406335 true] [17592186406336 1014 "Function Analysis and Applications" 13194139533505 17592186406335 true] [17592186406336 1015 #uuid "219db5a6-b013-55dc-84f6-a6af421dbaa2" 13194139533505 17592186406335 true] [17592186406336 1016 "Formulate and solve maximum and minimum problems using differential calculus." 13194139533505 17592186406335 true] [17592186406337 1013 5 13194139533505 17592186406335 true] [17592186406337 1014 "Function Analysis and Applications" 13194139533505 17592186406335 true] [17592186406337 1015 #uuid "6b02704b-77f0-59f1-b300-7d1ad5522589" 13194139533505 17592186406335 true] [17592186406337 1016 "Use derivatives to analyze motion, including relationships among position, velocity, speed, and acceleration." 13194139533505 17592186406335 true] [17592186406338 1013 6 13194139533505 17592186406335 true] [17592186406338 1014 "Function Analysis and Applications" 13194139533505 17592186406335 true] [17592186406338 1015 #uuid "794f739d-0e50-5e45-952a-ca590ac4b91e" 13194139533505 17592186406335 true] [17592186406338 1016 "Determine the shape and behavior of a graph using derivative information, including increasing and decreasing behavior, extrema, and concavity." 13194139533505 17592186406335 true] [17592186406339 1013 4 13194139533505 17592186406335 true] [17592186406339 1014 "Limits, Continuity, and Derivatives" 13194139533505 17592186406335 true] [17592186406339 1015 #uuid "8e0b28cf-8e52-5504-95a8-4e9b239c85a5" 13194139533505 17592186406335 true] [17592186406339 1016 "Differentiate single-variable functions using standard differentiation rules, including the chain rule." 13194139533505 17592186406335 true] [17592186406340 1013 1 13194139533505 17592186406335 true] [17592186406340 1014 "Limits, Continuity, and Derivatives" 13194139533505 17592186406335 true] [17592186406340 1015 #uuid "e11ac0b7-168a-53f3-9ca4-4dc90089617e" 13194139533505 17592186406335 true] [17592186406340 1016 "Evaluate limits using algebraic limit laws and interpret limiting behavior through graphical, numerical, and symbolic representations." 13194139533505 17592186406335 true] [17592186406341 1013 3 13194139533505 17592186406335 true] [17592186406341 1014 "Limits, Continuity, and Derivatives" 13194139533505 17592186406335 true] [17592186406341 1015 #uuid "e859a4bd-288c-5aef-9572-3dcf309d9852" 13194139533505 17592186406335 true] [17592186406341 1016 "Calculate average and instantaneous rates of change and explain how the derivative represents an instantaneous rate." 13194139533505 17592186406335 true] [17592186406342 1013 2 13194139533505 17592186406335 true] [17592186406342 1014 "Limits, Continuity, and Derivatives" 13194139533505 17592186406335 true] [17592186406342 1015 #uuid "f6479bab-c0ae-5920-bba4-64293ec407a7" 13194139533505 17592186406335 true] [17592186406342 1016 "Analyze the continuity of a single-variable function at a point or over an interval." 13194139533505 17592186406335 true] [17592186406343 1013 9 13194139533505 17592186406335 true] [17592186406343 1014 "Mathematical Modeling and Reasoning" 13194139533505 17592186406335 true] [17592186406343 1015 #uuid "0492ff25-34f8-58e0-8deb-997fcf89ad94" 13194139533505 17592186406335 true] [17592186406343 1016 "Evaluate the suitability of a mathematical approach and the validity of its conclusions, accounting for the assumptions used in constructing the model." 13194139533505 17592186406335 true] [17592186406344 1013 8 13194139533505 17592186406335 true] [17592186406344 1014 "Mathematical Modeling and Reasoning" 13194139533505 17592186406335 true] [17592186406344 1015 #uuid "5d9d7b2f-fee2-5bef-9ba6-2ce5add42a5d" 13194139533505 17592186406335 true] [17592186406344 1016 "Recognize situations that can be modeled mathematically, identify relevant quantities, and calculate or estimate the relationships needed to solve a problem." 13194139533505 17592186406335 true] [17592186406345 1013 9 13194139533505 17592186406335 true] [17592186406345 1014 "Differential Operators and Integral Theorems" 13194139533505 17592186406335 true] [17592186406345 1015 #uuid "8b26130c-3ade-59f1-b5cd-30c4e77a4ee2" 13194139533505 17592186406335 true] [17592186406345 1016 "Explain and apply the Divergence Theorem to connect flux through a closed surface with a volume integral." 13194139533505 17592186406335 true] [17592186406346 1013 10 13194139533505 17592186406335 true] [17592186406346 1014 "Differential Operators and Integral Theorems" 13194139533505 17592186406335 true] [17592186406346 1015 #uuid "11744f40-4941-5b05-bf74-21cff6dfbc86" 13194139533505 17592186406335 true] [17592186406346 1016 "Explain and apply Stokes’ Theorem to connect circulation around a boundary curve with a surface integral of curl." 13194139533505 17592186406335 true] [17592186406347 1013 8 13194139533505 17592186406335 true] [17592186406347 1014 "Differential Operators and Integral Theorems" 13194139533505 17592186406335 true] [17592186406347 1015 #uuid "68ca80d7-e11e-58c2-9696-309503e57498" 13194139533505 17592186406335 true] [17592186406347 1016 "Calculate divergence and curl in standard coordinate systems and interpret them in relation to flux and circulation." 13194139533505 17592186406335 true] [17592186406348 1013 5 13194139533505 17592186406335 true] [17592186406348 1014 "Line Integrals and Conservative Fields" 13194139533505 17592186406335 true] [17592186406348 1015 #uuid "9cd133e0-c54b-57f1-b597-4c2164a5bb07" 13194139533505 17592186406335 true] [17592186406348 1016 "Analyze conservative fields, find potential functions when they exist, and use path independence to evaluate line integrals." 13194139533505 17592186406335 true] [17592186406349 1013 4 13194139533505 17592186406335 true] [17592186406349 1014 "Line Integrals and Conservative Fields" 13194139533505 17592186406335 true] [17592186406349 1015 #uuid "da10e544-4158-5cc3-9189-961bf8e19bf4" 13194139533505 17592186406335 true] [17592186406349 1016 "Evaluate scalar and vector line integrals and interpret their use in calculating quantities such as mass, work, and circulation." 13194139533505 17592186406335 true] [17592186406350 1013 6 13194139533505 17592186406335 true] [17592186406350 1014 "Surface Integrals and Flux" 13194139533505 17592186406335 true] [17592186406350 1015 #uuid "c94983bd-7208-5af7-ab33-1ac7d94075a5" 13194139533505 17592186406335 true] [17592186406350 1016 "Set up and evaluate scalar surface integrals for quantities distributed over a surface, including mass or charge." 13194139533505 17592186406335 true] [17592186406351 1013 7 13194139533505 17592186406335 true] [17592186406351 1014 "Surface Integrals and Flux" 13194139533505 17592186406335 true] [17592186406351 1015 #uuid "25423c4b-02b3-5fec-9546-7129ca1e1f95" 13194139533505 17592186406335 true] [17592186406351 1016 "Calculate flux through an oriented surface and interpret the role of the field’s normal component." 13194139533505 17592186406335 true] [17592186406352 1013 2 13194139533505 17592186406335 true] [17592186406352 1014 "Vector Geome